  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nTonight, storms are moving across central IL. There is a low risk \n(but non-zero) for damaging winds within these storms, but the main \nconcern is the flash flooding risk. WPC has areas near and north of \nI-72 in a moderate risk (level 3 of 4) for excessive rainfall for \nthe overnight hours. The 00z sounding shows the environment having \nPWATs of 2.2-2.4 inches. Over the last few days, this area has \ngotten 3-6 inches of rain, with some locally higher amounts. The \nstorms passing through tonight are expected to drop another 2-5 \ninches with the LPMM suggesting some localized pockets of up to 10 \ninches. Flash flood guidance seems a bit generous (too high). If the \npockets of higher rain set up over where the ground is already \nextremely saturated, flash flooding is likely. \n\nFor the remainder of the day after this morning round of convection \nexits about 15-16z, there is a marginal risk for severe weather for \nadditional activity that will move through. It will be very similar \nto what has happened over the last few days. The next round of MCS \nactivity will move into central IL by late evening and will continue \ninto the overnight hours. The main concern will be damaging winds \nand the heavy rain potential.\n\nThere is another marginal risk for severe weather again tomorrow for \nareas south of a Schuyler to McLean County line with a similar \nenvironment in place as previous days. The forecast will continue to \nevolve as we get newer runs of the models. \n\nTemperatures remain hot and humid. The heat advisory for south of I-\n70 is valid through Sunday. Heat indices of 100-110 degrees are \nforecast through Sunday, but how far north these heat indices get \nwill depend solely on the convection location. Like yesterday for \nexample, the convection lingered further south longer than forecast, \nso heat indices struggled to hit triple digits. \n\nNext week may bring us a brief break in the active weather and the \nheat briefly. A high pressure area will set up over the Upper \nMississippi Valley / Great Lakes region by Monday, behind a cold \nfront that will move through central IL during the day. Temperatures \nstarting Monday into much of the next week will be mainly in the \nupper 70s to 80s for highs and 60s to low 70s for lows.",
